"""Own complete original-field readings, recorded before candidate or peer-note access."""
NOTES = {
('abudawud:2624','hi'): "Ibn Abbas supplies an actual revelation-occasion assertion about the source-named Abdullah and his expedition commission. The verse wording and its complete parenthetical translation are integral quoted content. Retain the printed participant-name/lineage qualification as this source's identity wording without repairing it from outside knowledge; only the explicit surah/verse locator is apparatus. This is the Companion's reported occasion assertion, not an independently authenticated historical finding.",
('abudawud:2624','ur'): "The entire Urdu independently supplies the quoted verse, rendering, source-named Abdullah identity and the claim that the verse concerned his expedition commission. Its parenthetical Abdullah-bin-Hudhafa identification distinguishes the participant and should remain source-rendered attribution, while the exact Nasaa59 locator remains context. Preserve the source's adjacent Qays-bin-Adi wording without normalizing the name chain or importing Hindi's phrasing.",
('abudawud:2629','hi'): "Muadh ibn Anas supplies his expedition participation, blocked camp/routes, the dispatched announcer and the stated no-jihad consequence. The final Hindi repeats damaged camp/blocking language and lacks part of Urdu's clearer object wording; preserve that defective finite source clause without completion. No detached compiler comment occurs in this own field.",
('abudawud:2629','ur'): "Read the complete camp-constriction report and the Prophet's dispatched announcement in Urdu. Preserve both alternatives, narrowing camp space or blocking a road, and the supplied consequence. The embedded numbered footnote is apparatus only. Do not use this clearer Urdu to fill Hindi's broken ending.",
('abudawud:2630','hi'): "The source supplies one finite observation: Muadh says they undertook a campaign with Allah's Prophet. The following narrator's same-meaning pointer supplies no additional report body and remains source context. Admit the actual short participation clause under Muadh's printed attribution without importing the preceding occurrence's camp incident.",
('abudawud:2630','ur'): "This own Urdu similarly gives a finite campaign-participation statement, then a bare same-meaning narration pointer. The first statement can stand as the source's expressly abbreviated observation; the pointer cannot justify filling another account. Retain the full original pointer as context and do not infer the campaign's name or details.",
('abudawud:2635','hi'): "The complete Isam Muzani report contains a commission to an expedition and the actual conditional instruction not to kill anyone upon seeing a mosque or hearing an adhan. Both alternatives, the nobody scope and the source's reporter spelling remain exact. No extra explanation or source-only tail needs admission.",
('abudawud:2635','ur'): "The Urdu independently supplies Isam's expedition commission and both mosque/adhan conditions of the no-killing instruction. Preserve the coherent short attribution and all finite wording, with no outside completion or authenticity upgrade.",
('abudawud:2638','hi'): "Salama reports Abu Bakr's commissioned leadership, the night attack, the actual repeated slogan and his own seven-households killing statement. Preserve this as Salama's account, not a claim that all his acts are direct Prophetic commands. The code synonym explains the word for slogan and remains lexical context; the actual slogan and number remain exact, including the source's household wording.",
('abudawud:2638','ur'): "Read the entire Salama narrative independently. The repeated am-it slogan and the seven-households statement are actual own-field content; the parenthetic code definition and both numbered footnote markers are apparatus. Retain Abu Bakr's commissioned role and the source's own actions/numbers without canonical correction.",
('abudawud:2643','hi'): "Usama's complete account includes the commission, surrounding a man, his confession, the killing, Usama's fear-of-sword explanation, the Messenger's repeated challenge and Usama's final wish. The despite-this, repeated fear-of-sword alternative and rather-for-Islam parentheses materially frame the action and alternative intention; retain them as this source's rendering rather than claim their verbatim historical wording. Preserve every question and the final repeated-warning effect.",
('abudawud:2643','ur'): "The entire Urdu independently supplies Usama's own scene and dialogue. The parenthetical despite-this connector and both intention-alternative scopes belong with the actual narrative/question, while the two numbered footnote glyphs are source apparatus. Do not turn Usama's explanation into the Messenger's assertion or erase the source-rendered alternative intention wording.",
('abudawud:2645','hi'): "Jarir reports the Khatham expedition, the people's attempted protection by prostration, the hasty killing, half compensation, repudiation statement and the question/answer about the two fires. The explicit yani Islam/kufr parenthesis is a conceptual explanation of the metaphor and remains context; surrounding actual answer stays. Preserve the exact half amount and the source's own reported condition.",
('abudawud:2645','ur'): "The entire Urdu has the same independently supplied incident, half-diya response and fire question/answer, followed by Abu Dawud's transmitter-list/omitted-Jarir criticism. That final route criticism is context, along with the body footnotes and explicit Islam/kufr synonym gloss. Keep the actual dialogue and finite condition unchanged, without treating the compiler criticism as another event.",
('abudawud:2649','hi'): "Khabbab's full visit/complaint account includes the cloak setting, the two help/prayer questions, sitting and reddened face, the earlier sufferers' endurance, oath, promised safe journey and impatience rebuke. The parenthetical complaint domain and hearing-this transition materially specify the complaint and response sequence and remain source-rendered framing. Preserve the defective Hindi tendon token and the exact places, sheep/wolf exception and religious claims as attributed speech.",
('abudawud:2649','ur'): "Read the whole Urdu independently, including the complaint about the unbelievers' dominance and the upon-hearing-this transition. These supply actual domain/sequence, not detached lexical definitions. Preserve both earlier-suffering descriptions, the oath, Sanaa/Hadramawt journey and the wolf-for-sheep exception under the source's reported speech without adding outside history.",
('abudawud:2654','hi'): "Salama ibn al-Akwa supplies a full expedition account: the red-camel visitor, meal, observation of weakness, departure, pursuit, relative camel positions, killing, return and final allocation dialogue. The parenthesis that people learned he was a spy is an actual narrative-cause/participant assessment within this source and remains qualified source rendering. Preserve every reported movement and attribution as historical narration, not independent factual or normative endorsement, and do not normalize damaged Hindi words.",
('abudawud:2654','ur'): "The whole Urdu independently describes the visitor and pursuit, Salama's killing/return and the people's answer identifying him before the Messenger's allocation. The spy-discovery parenthesis materially supplies the stated causal transition and remains. Only the final numbered footnote is apparatus; all source positions, horse/camel distinctions as printed, speaker changes and the final allocation scope remain exact.",
('abudawud:2658','hi'): "The source-named बारे reports the Hunayn encounter, a plural group's scattering and the Messenger dismounting and walking. The explicit yani defeated-and-fled parenthesis explains the already supplied scattering term and remains lexical/interpretive context. Preserve the printed pronoun's scope without deciding from another version which group it names, and retain the defective source narrator spelling.",
('abudawud:2658','ur'): "The full Urdu contains the encounter, scattering and dismounting observation. Its explicit yani expansion explains the scattering word rather than supply a separately framed event; retain that explanation as source context while keeping the complete finite observation and its own pronouns unchanged.",
('abudawud:2668','hi'): "The complete source report says an unnamed woman was found killed in an unnamed expedition during the Messenger's lifetime, followed by his rebuke concerning killing women and children. Preserve the actual scope, unknown occasion and printed Abdullah-bin-Umr wording. Do not identify the woman, battle or a different narrator from external memory.",
('abudawud:2668','ur'): "The own Urdu independently supplies the discovered slain woman and the Messenger's rebuke against killing women and children. It is a complete finite report with no extra compiler passage. Preserve the exact campaign indefiniteness and both protected groups without adding absent circumstances.",
('abudawud:2674','hi'): "Abu Hurayrah reports an actual commission and the explicitly introduced conditional fragment if you find so-and-so and so-and-so. Preserve that abbreviated supplied condition while marking its absent consequent; the following same-meaning pointer is context and cannot authorize importing an order from another occurrence. Targets and unstated result remain unknown.",
('abudawud:2674','ur'): "Read the entire short Urdu independently. The Messenger's dispatch and printed if-you-find condition are actual supplied framing/abbreviated speech, even though no consequence follows. Keep these exact fragments under their printed attribution and preserve the final same-meaning pointer as context, without identifying the unnamed persons or filling the missing order.",
('abudawud:2678','hi'): "Jundab ibn Muqis gives his expedition participation, the source-named commander/target/place, the encounter with Harith, Harith's conversion-intent claim and the soldiers' two conditional replies before binding him. Retain the whole finite account with nested speakers made explicit; the soldiers' detention reasoning is not relabelled as direct Prophetic speech. Preserve every printed name and the one-day/one-night period without historical correction.",
('abudawud:2678','ur'): "The complete Urdu independently supplies the expedition commission and the actual Harith/troop dialogue. Preserve both Muslim/not-Muslim alternatives, the time limit and the final binding act under Jundab's account. The Prophet's opening commission does not make the later soldiers' words his utterance.",
('abudawud:2688','hi'): "Anas reports eighty men descending at Fajr, their intended attack, capture without resistance, release and a revelation-occasion assertion with actual verse wording and translation. Preserve the finite event, number and recitation text; the parenthetic numeral merely duplicates the already printed eighty and the explicit Fath24 label is source apparatus. Keep the damaged place spelling and do not replace it from Urdu.",
('abudawud:2688','ur'): "The own Urdu contains the same independently stated eighty-men incident and actual revelation quotation/rendering. Preserve the narrated intention, capture/release and verse words; the to-the-end-of-the-verse pointer supplies no further text and remains context, as does the explicit Fath24 locator. Do not fill the unquoted remainder of the verse.",
('abudawud:2690','hi'): "Ibn Abbas explicitly relays Umar's Badr/ransom and revelation account, ending with the stated later permissibility of spoils. Keep the actual source quotation/rendering and all its broken words without adding the omitted verse wording. The parenthetic unbeliever referent materially specifies the object in the source rendering and remains; the explicit Anfal locator and the lexical definition of the spoils word are context. Do not present the damaged translation as repaired canonical wording.",
('abudawud:2690','ur'): "The full Urdu independently relays Umar through Ibn Abbas, then gives the source's beginning/end quotation markers, its supplied verse rendering and the later spoils statement. Preserve the bounded citation as the source's own abbreviated identification, without supplying its absent intervening quotation. The unbeliever-object parenthesis is material source-rendered scope, while the explicit Anfal67-68 locator is apparatus. Keep the fuller Urdu separate from Hindi's damaged shorter wording.",
('abudawud:2696','hi'): "The actual report attributes separation of a bondwoman and child to Ali, followed by the Messenger's prohibition and cancellation of the sale. Abu Dawud's subsequent non-meeting assertion and death/battle dates form transmitter-chronology criticism supporting that source apparatus, not a separate narrated scene here. Preserve the complete critical tail as context and keep the finite opening report's own attribution unchanged.",
('abudawud:2696','ur'): "Read the complete Urdu, including all appended Maimun/Ali chronology and dates. Select only the finite separation/prohibition/sale-reversal account; retain the following explicitly introduced compiler criticism and its chronological explanations as context. The source's numbers and names remain exact, with no external chronology repair.",
('abudawud:2698','hi'): "The opening Ibn Umar account supplies the slave's flight, the military outcome, return attributed to the Messenger and exclusion from spoils distribution. Abu Dawud then reports another version's finite alternative that Khalid ibn Walid returned him. This is actual variant action/actor wording, not bare grading: retain it as a separate source-qualified transmitted-variant component in the same event family, without choosing an actor or inventing another incident.",
('abudawud:2698','ur'): "The entire Urdu independently contains the main return/distribution account and the expressly attributed other-transmission clause naming Khalid as the returning actor. Preserve both as source versions of one account, with Abu Dawud's version-notice framing retained and qualified. Do not make Khalid's action direct Prophetic speech or silently reconcile the alternative attribution.",
('tirmidhi:2622','hi'): "The source contains two actual attributed statements: Abdullah ibn Shaqiq's report of the Companions' view of abandoning prayer, then Tirmidhi's explicitly heard Abu Musab doctrinal/legal saying. The second is a named substantive scholarly statement, distinct from a grade or lexical explanation; retain it under Abu Musab, not as Prophetic or Companion speech, in a separate report family. The qawl definition parenthesis is lexical context. Hindi omits Urdu's explicit repentance-success/otherwise contrast; preserve that defect and do not supply the missing conditional. This is historical attribution, not endorsement or contemporary instruction.",
('tirmidhi:2622','ur'): "Read both the Companions-view report and the explicitly heard Abu Musab statement in full. The latter independently supplies an if-he-repents/otherwise contrast absent from Hindi and stays under its own scholar attribution, separate from the preceding community report. Only the explicit yani verbal-confession synonym is lexical context. Preserve the normative quotation as reported source history, without making it Prophetic speech, a present legal ruling or an authenticated endorsement.",
('tirmidhi:2665','hi'): "Abu Said's report supplies an actual request for permission to write and the refused permission. The initial-Islam temporal parenthesis and hadith object specification materially narrow when/what was requested and remain source-rendered scope. The following Tirmidhi other-route transmission notice is compiler context, including its damaged Hindi tail. Do not turn this qualified occasion into an unrestricted statement about writing all knowledge.",
('tirmidhi:2665','ur'): "The complete Urdu independently supplies the early-Islam setting, writing-hadith request and refusal. Both parenthetic temporal/object restrictions remain with the actual account; the numbered footnote and the later Zayd/other-chain/Hammam notice are apparatus. Preserve the source's exact qualifications and separate that route discussion from the report.",
('tirmidhi:2680','hi'): "The actual report is expressly marked marfu through Abu Hurayrah and predicts extended study journeys with no greater scholar than Medina's scholar. Keep its own stated attribution and universal comparison without naming that scholar. The appended grade and majority-Malik identification are interpretive compiler context, not supplied Prophetic identity or a separate narrated event.",
('tirmidhi:2680','ur'): "The whole Urdu independently supplies the marfu study-journey report; its parenthetic anywhere qualification materially scopes the negative comparison and remains. The five numbered sections thereafter grade or interpret the anonymous Medina-scholar referent, including named hearing/question frames and genealogy; these are explanatory apparatus and all remain context. Do not fill Malik, Umari or another identity into the selected report.",
('tirmidhi:2683','hi'): "Yazid's own Hindi has a damaged hearing/fear/request transition but supplies his two-period hadith concern and the explicitly introduced answer about fear of Allah concerning known things. Preserve the finite fragments and omitted links without reconstruction from Urdu. The awwal/akhir parenthesis merely re-labels the already contrasted earlier/later pair and remains lexical context; the actual both-periods scope stays outside it.",
('tirmidhi:2683','ur'): "The entire Urdu contains Yazid's fear of forgetting earlier reports, his request and the Messenger's reply. The any-such-saying synonym and awwal/akhir apposition repeat already supplied nouns/domains and remain context; the meaningful request/answer is retained. The footnote and subsequent disconnected-chain/mursal/name commentary are compiler apparatus, not another report body.",
('tirmidhi:2690','hi'): "Abu Said's full account contains Abu Musa's three requests, Umar's explicitly inner responses, the porter exchange, the demand for proof, Abu Musa's appeal with an embedded Prophetic permission instruction, the listeners' response, Abu Said's support/testimony and Umar's final lack-of-knowledge admission. Preserve all three in-the-heart qualifications, the further-waiting scope, hearing-this transition and final Abu-Said actor identification as source-rendered narrative. The parenthetic permission-seeking wording renders the actually quoted Arabic/transliteration and stays. Distinguish the reception dialogue, bounded embedded instruction and resumed testimony within the account; do not flatten all voices into the Prophet's speech or manufacture separate canonical incidents.",
('tirmidhi:2690','ur'): "The entire Urdu independently supplies the same multi-voice reception/testimony account, including three inner-speech qualifications, additional waiting, the actual quotation/rendering, hearing-this response and the parenthetic visitor identification. These meaning-bearing scopes remain selected as this source's rendering. The numbered footnote and all subsequent grading, narrator-name/kunya explanations, other-route and chapter-parallel notice are compiler context. Keep the exact three-request conditions, Abu Said's own supportive utterance and Umar's final acknowledgement under their actual speakers.",
('tirmidhi:2694','hi'): "Abu Umamah reports the actual two-person greeting question and the answer identifying the one nearer to Allah. The source's parenthetic he-will-initiate is a finite answer predicate completing its own question/answer relation; preserve it as source-rendered response scope without claiming that parenthesis is verbatim Prophetic wording. The appended Tirmidhi grade/transmitter criticism remains context, including the damaged Hindi link.",
('tirmidhi:2694','ur'): "Read the full Urdu question and response. Preserve the finite parenthetic he-will-initiate under this source's rendering of the answer, while the footnote and the following numbered grade/Bukhari transmitter appraisal remain apparatus. Do not turn that appraisal into another narrated greeting report or normalize its source names.",
}
